WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.929
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch and

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.390
I'm musician-in-residence
at Yale University's

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.353
Fortunoff Video Archive for Holocaust

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:42.790
Testimonies.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.630
This video series is
designed as a companion

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.759
to our album Where Is Our Homeland, which

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.759
is the first volume in the archives ongoing

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.460
effort to present songs and poems

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.039
performed by survivors in testimonies given

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.529
to the Fortunoff Archive over the last

00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:59.530
forty years.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.889
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.620
Archive's website,
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.859
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.987
Songs from Testimonies,

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.329
Volume One was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.508
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble, featuring

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.928
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.459
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua Camp

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.920
on the accordion, keyboards and guitar,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.052
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass, and myself

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.010
on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.955
The songs in this
collection differ vastly in

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.873
terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.400
background, language,
and many other aspects,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.609
but collectively they
create a multidimensional

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.129
picture of Jewish life before and

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.350
during World War II.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.100
Irene S. was born in 1925

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.740
in Berezhany, or Brzezany, Galicia,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.300
now Ukraine, and was raised in Grudziadz,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.580
Poland.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.689
She was a survivor of the Bialystok ghetto

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.778
and several concentration camps, including

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.429
Majdanek, Treblinka and Auschwitz.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.940
Irene was a slave laborer in Germany

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.940
and was liberated by the Americans in

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:06.940
Kaunitz.

00:02:07.288 --> 00:02:09.038
By the time the war broke out,

00:02:09.358 --> 00:02:11.487
Irene already was an
accomplished singer and

00:02:11.488 --> 00:02:12.488
songwriter.

00:02:13.118 --> 00:02:15.877
In her testimony, she
recalls singing existing

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:18.637
songs and composing new ones together

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.500
with her young fellow prisoners from Poland

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.860
and the Soviet Union.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.815
The poem "Sakhki,
Sakhki," otherwise known as

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.649
"Ani Ma'amin," "I Believe," was

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.460
written by the poet, translator, physician

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.020
and writer Shaul Tchernichovsky in

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.959
Odessa, Ukraine, then Russian Empire,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.459
in 1892.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.179
Tchernichovsky was
born in Ukraine to a modern

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.789
religious family, and studied languages

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.249
in Odessa, where he became involved in

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.509
Zionist activities.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.940
Tchernichovsky served
as a doctor in the Russian

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.380
army during World War I, and then

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.340
pursuing his dream, he repatriated

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.099
or emigrated to the land of Israel

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.340
to, then Palestine in 1931.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.799
"I Believe" is one of
Tchernichovsky’s very early

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.649
poems, permeated by idealistic feelings.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.080
The young poet unites
the ideals of liberty,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.190
fraternity, peace, and Zionist ideals,

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.398
the Jewish people's hope and aspiration to

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.580
freely live in their ancestral land,

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:21.700
the land of Israel.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.610
It is really moving and symbolic

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.972
how all these ideals
strongly resonated with the

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.400
feelings of the young inmates at

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.139
the ghettos and camps among which was

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.629
Irene. The songs like this helped them

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.330
survive and make it to the Liberation Day.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.980
Ki od nafshi dror sho'efet--

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.200
if there was still freedom to my soul.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.420
Because my soul still longs for freedom

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.780
I have not sold it for a golden calf.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.580
Because I still believe in humanity

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.140
And in its spirit, a strong spirit.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.540
Because I still believe in humanity

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.860
And in its spirit, a strong spirit.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.580
Laugh, laugh at these dreams –

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.780
This is me, the dreamer, speaking

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.900
Laugh because I still believe in humanity,

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.940
Because I still believe in you.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:06.900
Laugh because I still believe in humanity,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:13.100
Because I still believe in you.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.620
Because my soul still longs for freedom,

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.780
I have not sold it for a golden calf.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.060
Because I still believe in humanity

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:48.140
And in its spirit, a strong spirit.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:54.020
Because I still believe in humanity

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.500
And in its spirit, a strong spirit.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:18.300
This spirit will cast off
the shackles of falsehood,

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.500
And will be uplifted.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:29.620
No worker shall die of hunger;

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.420
Freedom for the soul, bread for the poor.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:40.540
No worker shall die of hunger;

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:48.300
Freedom for the soul, bread for the poor.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.199
In her testimony, Irene recalls

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.890
that while being
transported from the Bialystok

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.450
ghetto to an unknown destination,

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:17.340
she and other young people in the cattle

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.089
car began singing.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:21.980
They sang, as she says, patriotic

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.519
songs, Zionist songs.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.980
We were singing ourselves to go to

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.340
die, she says.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.227
One of these songs whose melodies she wrote

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.339
herself was in Russian because

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.980
she says the boy who wrote the words

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.509
didn't know any Yiddish.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.199
They did not sing for long because

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.111
the guards on the train shouted at them

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.570
to stop and fired into the cattle car,

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.980
wounding Irene and killing one

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.140
of the lads.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.249
The transport passed by the extermination

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.250
camp, Treblinka.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.809
And she remembers seeing vast fields

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:02.240
covered with ashes and bones.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.580
But they went on to Lublin and she

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.600
subsequently was taken to the nearby

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.460
concentration and extermination camp

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:12.660
Majdanek.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.660
Yet Irene also remembers that the young

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.429
men and women in the cattle car sang

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.009
another song and she beautifully

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.200
sings a few verses of it.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.840
The song is known as "Sakhki, Sakhki,"

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.859
although its original name is "I Believe,"

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.499
"Ani Ma'amin." Irene

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.840
does not mention the name of the song

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:38.300
and erroneously attributed to

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:39.829
Hayim Nahman Bialik.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.929
In fact, it is based on a poem

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.019
written by Shaul Tchernichovsky, another

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.449
important Jewish Zionist poet from

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.789
Ukraine who, like Bialik,

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.220
ended up living in Palestine.

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:57.220
Tchernichovsky was
born in a small Ukrainian

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.590
village in 1875

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.249
and wrote "Sakhki, Sakhki" in 1894,

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.769
when he was merely 19 years old.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.550
At the time, he was living in Odessa

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.430
and already knew both Russian and Hebrew.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.639
He subsequently also learned German,

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.459
English and French, as well as

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.009
ancient Greek and Latin.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.580
A man of great talent and energy, he

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.180
studied medicine at the Universities of

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.950
Heidelberg, Lausanne and Kiev.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.300
While studying at
Heidelberg, Tchernichovsky

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.309
also married the anarchist Russian

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.973
aristocrat Melania Karlovna,

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:41.279
a Christian of Polish, German

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:42.849
and Ukrainian descent.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.840
Their marriage lasted until his death

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.580
in 1943.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.629
Tchernichovsky served as a military doctor

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.850
in the Russian army in World War I

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.969
and emigrated with his wife and daughter to

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.410
Palestine in 1931.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.699
He spent the last years of his life,

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.340
having contracted leukemia, in the

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:08.119
Greek patriarchs' residence in

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.299
San Simon in Jerusalem, along

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.209
with his wife, who had become a devout

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:15.410
Orthodox Christian.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:19.369
Tchernichovsky is
remembered as one of the most

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:22.400
influential poets of his generation,

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:25.520
and his works betray the influence

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:27.780
of his Jewish heritage, his love

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.719
for biblical figures and the impact

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.150
of world literature.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:36.089
"Sakhki Sakhki," which was included

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.939
in Tchernichovsky's first poetry collection

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:41.910
published in 1898, combines

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.439
a youthful belief in humanism, socialism,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.539
the fraternity of nations, friendship,

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:49.540
and Zionism.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.919
It was very popular among the Zionist

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.710
socialist youth of Irene's generation,

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.679
and it is no wonder that she

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:01.349
and her friends sang it in

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:02.630
their transport to hell.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.300
My own mother, who was just a

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:09.069
year older than Irene, also

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:11.700
loved that poem, and I still recall

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.969
her singing it in my childhood.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.580
But the song has remained very popular,

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.609
not least in Israel, and it is still

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.700
sung in youth movements, public

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.439
events, and social gatherings, and

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.060
has been performed by an array of popular

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:28.980
singers.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.309
There are several
other interesting twists to

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.979
the story of "Sakhki, Sakhki," whose

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.500
original title, "Ani Ma'amin,"

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:40.589
refers to a pronouncement in the Jewish

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.839
prayer book, Siddur, based

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.220
on the writings of Maimonides,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.620
which expresses the
thirteen main principles

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:49.930
of Jewish faith.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.020
The words of Ani Ma'amin were made

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.069
into a popular Hasidic tune.

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:58.246
The well-known cantor, Azriel Fastag,

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:01.729
is said to have sung this Hasidic tune

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.420
on the train to Treblinka, where he was

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:05.550
murdered.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.959
But unlike this Hasidic tune, the melody

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.569
of "Sakhki, Sakhki"
sounds distinctly Russian.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.810
Officially, the composer of this song

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:17.900
is said to have been Tuvia Shlonsky,

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:20.420
a Zionist from a Hasidic family

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.270
in Ukraine, and the father

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:26.020
of the influential Hebrew poet, editor,

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.620
and translator Avraham Shlonsky,

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.729
one of my own father's early mentors,

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.130
when he began his own literary career.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.050
It is, however, quite likely that

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.119
the tune of "Sakhki, Sakhki" originated

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:43.969
in a Russian folk song from the Don region.

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:47.140
One indication of that is that the very

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.240
same melody was used for another song

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.509
written by Rabbi Avrahom Eliyahu Kaplan,

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.450
a major Orthodox figure from Lithuania,

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.470
which he wrote as a 17 year old lad.

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.499
The song, which expresses the spiritual

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.139
torment of a young soul and

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.259
its longing for God is officially titled

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.779
"Prayer," "Tfila." But it

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:14.389
is known and sung to this day under

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.400
the name "The Sun Has Set,"

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:19.140
"Shak'a Chama." Since it

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:21.420
is unlikely the Rabbi Kaplan also

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:23.870
wrote the tune, we can assume

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.700
that it was put to music using

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.780
the same popular Russian tune that

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.100
was used for "Sakhki, Sakhki,"

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.889
a Russian song that served

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:38.189
to animate both a Hasidic longing

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.620
for God, and a Zionist Socialist

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.739
longing for equality, fraternity,

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.454
and a new Jewish generation in Eretz

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:47.860
Israel.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.639
One can find a beautiful performance

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.540
of "The Sun Has Set" by the Hasidic

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.569
singer Abish Brodt on YouTube.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:00.460
The popularity of
"Sakhki, Sakhki" in Israel

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:03.700
to this day found another remarkable

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.699
expression in 2004

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.560
during a discussion in the Israeli

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.999
Knesset on the collective rights of,

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:14.460
of the Arab population in Israel.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:17.900
At one point in the lengthy debate, the

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.766
Arab member of Knesset, Mohammad Barakeh,

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.761
proposed the Tchernichovsky "Sakhki,

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:26.300
Sakhki" would be a more appropriate

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.019
national anthem than the current one,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.780
which speaks primarily about the

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:33.900
longing of Jews to Zion.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:36.300
The proposal was rejected,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:38.900
but it is a tantalizing

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.180
idea.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.380
Perhaps one day Tchernichovsky's verse,

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:47.220
"For my soul still longs for freedom/I

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:50.100
have not sold it to the Golden Calf/

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:53.900
For I have still faith in humanity/And

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.589
in its spirit, fierce and

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:59.140
bold," sung by Jewish youth

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:02.109
on the way to Treblinka, will still

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:04.300
become the national anthem of

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.910
both Jews and Palestinians

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:09.010
in the state of Israel.

